🧠 Core Concepts
Partitions
A partition is a logical division of a physical disk. It allows you to separate data, install multiple OSes, or apply different filesystems.

Tool	Style	Best For
fdisk	MBR (DOS)	Disks < 2 TB, simple setups
parted	GPT	Disks > 2 TB, modern systems
bash
# List partitions
fdisk -l
parted -l

# Interactive partitioning with fdisk
fdisk /dev/sdb

# Create a GPT partition table with parted
parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary ext4 0% 100%
Filesystems
A filesystem defines how data is stored and retrieved on a partition.

Filesystem	Max File Size	Max Volume	Journaling	Best For
ext4	16 TB	1 EB	Yes	General purpose, stable
XFS	8 EB	8 EB	Yes	Large files, high performance
Btrfs	16 EB	16 EB	Yes (CoW)	Snapshots, advanced features
tmpfs	RAM	RAM	No	Temporary, in-memory
bash
# Create an ext4 filesystem
mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1

# Create an XFS filesystem
mkfs.xfs /dev/sdb1

# Check filesystem type
blkid /dev/sdb1
Mount Points
A mount point is a directory where a filesystem is attached to the tree.

bash
# Mount a filesystem
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# Unmount
umount /mnt/data

# Show all mounts
mount | column -t

# Show mounts with filesystem type and usage
df -hT
/etc/fstab
/etc/fstab defines filesystems to mount automatically at boot.

Format:

text
<device>  <mount point>  <filesystem>  <options>  <dump>  <pass>
Example:

text
UUID=1234-ABCD  /data  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2
Field	Meaning
device	UUID, LABEL, or path
mount point	Where to mount
filesystem	ext4, xfs, etc.
options	defaults, noatime, ro, etc.
dump	0 (ignore)
pass	fsck order: 0=don't check, 1=root, 2=others
Always test with mount -a before rebooting!

bash
# Get UUID
blkid /dev/sdb1

# Test fstab
mount -a
Inodes
An inode stores metadata about a file (permissions, owner, size, pointers to data blocks) — but not the filename.

Every file consumes one inode.

Inodes are allocated when the filesystem is created.

Running out of inodes means you cannot create new files even if disk space is free.

bash
# Check inode usage
df -i

# See inode number of a file
ls -i file.txt

# Count inodes used in a directory
find /path -xdev -printf '%i\n' | wc -l
Disk Full vs Inode Full
Symptom	Check	Cause	Fix
Disk full	df -h shows 100%	Large files	Delete/compress files, extend disk
Inode full	df -i shows 100%	Many small files	Delete small files, increase inodes (recreate FS)
Disk full:

bash
df -h
du -sh /* | sort -rh | head -10
Inode full:

bash
df -i
find / -xdev -type f | wc -l
find / -xdev -type d -size +100M  # directories with many files
XFS vs ext4
Feature	ext4	XFS
Max file size	16 TB	8 EB
Max filesystem	1 EB	8 EB
Journaling	Yes	Yes
Resize	Grow & shrink	Grow only (no shrink)
Performance	Good general purpose	Excellent for large files & parallel I/O
Inodes	Fixed at creation (can be dynamic)	Dynamic allocation
Best for	Root filesystem, general use	Data volumes, databases, large files
Note: XFS cannot be shrunk. Plan accordingly.

LVM Basics
LVM (Logical Volume Manager) adds a layer of abstraction between physical disks and filesystems.

Stack:

text
Physical Disk → Partition → PV (Physical Volume) → VG (Volume Group) → LV (Logical Volume) → Filesystem → Mount
Benefits:

Resize volumes online

Combine multiple disks into one pool

Snapshots

Easy migration

Commands:

Command	Purpose
pvcreate	Create physical volume
pvs	List PVs
vgcreate	Create volume group
vgs	List VGs
lvcreate	Create logical volume
lvs	List LVs
lvextend	Extend LV
resize2fs / xfs_growfs	Resize filesystem
lvremove	Remove LV
🛠 Command Reference
df — Disk space usage
bash
df -h              # Human-readable
df -hT             # Show filesystem type
df -i              # Inode usage
df -h /data        # Specific mount
du — Directory disk usage
bash
du -sh /var/log           # Total size of directory
du -sh /* | sort -rh | head -10   # Top 10 largest directories
du -ah /data | sort -rh | head -20 # Top 20 largest files/dirs
lsblk — List block devices
bash
lsblk
lsblk -f           # Show filesystem type and UUID
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MOUNTPOINT
mount / umount
bash
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data
umount /mnt/data
mount | column -t
mount -a           # Mount all from fstab
blkid — Block device UUIDs
bash
blkid
blkid /dev/sdb1
fdisk — MBR partitioning
bash
fdisk -l
fdisk /dev/sdb
# Interactive: n (new), p (primary), w (write)
parted — GPT partitioning
bash
parted -l
parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary ext4 0% 100%
LVM Commands
bash
pvcreate /dev/sdb1
pvs
vgcreate vg_data /dev/sdb1
vgs
lvcreate -L 10G -n lv_data vg_data
lvs
mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg_data/lv_data
mount /dev/vg_data/lv_data /data

# Extend
lvextend -L +5G /dev/vg_data/lv_data
resize2fs /dev/vg_data/lv_data      # ext4
# xfs_growfs /data                   # XFS
🧪 Lab: Create a Virtual Disk and Build the Full Stack
Objective: Create a virtual disk, partition it, build LVM on top, format, and mount it.

Step 1: Create a virtual disk (loop device)
bash
# Create a 2GB file to act as a disk
dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/vdisk.img bs=1M count=2048

# Attach it as a loop device
losetup -fP /tmp/vdisk.img
losetup -a
You should see something like /dev/loop0.

Step 2: Partition the disk
bash
fdisk /dev/loop0
# n → new partition
# p → primary
# 1 → partition number
# Enter → default first sector
# Enter → default last sector (uses whole disk)
# t → type
# 8e → Linux LVM
# w → write
Verify:

bash
fdisk -l /dev/loop0
Step 3: Create Physical Volume (PV)
bash
pvcreate /dev/loop0p1
pvs
Step 4: Create Volume Group (VG)
bash
vgcreate vg_lab /dev/loop0p1
vgs
Step 5: Create Logical Volume (LV)
bash
lvcreate -L 1G -n lv_data vg_lab
lvs
Step 6: Create filesystem
bash
mkfs.ext4 /dev/vg_lab/lv_data
blkid /dev/vg_lab/lv_data
Step 7: Mount
bash
mkdir -p /data
mount /dev/vg_lab/lv_data /data
df -h /data
Step 8: Test persistence
bash
# Add to fstab
echo "UUID=$(blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/vg_lab/lv_data) /data ext4 defaults 0 2" >> /etc/fstab

# Test
umount /data
mount -a
df -h /data
Step 9: Cleanup (optional)
bash
umount /data
lvremove /dev/vg_lab/lv_data
vgremove vg_lab
pvremove /dev/loop0p1
losetup -d /dev/loop0
rm /tmp/vdisk.img
🔥 Troubleshooting: Simulate /data at 100% and Fix It
Scenario 1: Disk space full
Simulate:

bash
# Fill /data with a large file
dd if=/dev/zero of=/data/bigfile bs=1M count=900
df -h /data
Diagnose:

bash
df -h /data
du -sh /data/* | sort -rh | head -10
lsof +L1   # Check for deleted but open files
Fix:

Delete or compress large files:

bash
rm /data/bigfile
Or extend the LV:

bash
lvextend -L +1G /dev/vg_lab/lv_data
resize2fs /dev/vg_lab/lv_data
df -h /data
Scenario 2: Inode full
Simulate:

bash
# Create many small files
mkdir /data/many
for i in {1..100000}; do touch /data/many/file$i; done
df -i /data
Diagnose:

bash
df -i /data
find /data -xdev -type f | wc -l
find /data -xdev -type d -size +10M
Fix:

Delete unnecessary files:

bash
rm -rf /data/many
If inodes are exhausted on ext4, you cannot increase them without recreating the filesystem. Consider migrating to XFS (dynamic inodes) or a new filesystem with more inodes.

Debug Chain for Storage
text
df -h / df -i
  ↓
du -sh /* | sort -rh
  ↓
find / -xdev -size +1G
  ↓
lsof +L1 (deleted open files)
  ↓
Extend LV or clean up
